{"question":"How does rally scoring work in a Picklebreaker?","concept":{"leagues":[],"division":null,"question":"how does rally scoring work in a picklebreaker?","kind":"scoring","phase":"picklebreaker","query":"Picklebreaker Match Format"},"selected":[{"id":"ce9b014e-e3d3-43b0-86bf-9523481d4e84","page":16,"score":0.5598,"content":"PICKLEBREAKER™ GAME OVERVIEW\nThe Picklebreaker™ is our league’s tiebreaker format, similar to Major League Pickleball’s (MLP)\nDreambreaker. It is used when a match is tied after the day’s regular games. Unlike the MLP\nversion, our Picklebreaker uses doubles rather than singles.\nHere is how it works:\nThe Format: The Picklebreaker™ is a doubles game played to either 15 or 25 points, depending\non the league. It always uses Rally Scoring, where every rally/serve earns a point regardless of\nwhich team served. Refer to the league-specific rules for Rally Scoring, total points, and win-by\nrequirements.\nThe Rotations: Each team’s designated doubles pairs shall enter the game in the order\nestablished by their captain prior to the start of play. Both doubles pairs currently on the\ncourt shall remain in play until the combined game score reaches the next rotation\nthreshold, at which time both pairs shall rotate out and the next designated doubles pair from\neach team shall enter.\nFor games played to 15 points, two (2) designated doubles pairs from each team shall\nparticipate in the rotation, with substitutions occurring after every four (4) combined points\nscored.\nFor games played to 25 points, six (6) designated doubles pairs from each team shall\nparticipate in the rotation, with substitutions occurring after every four (4) combined points\nscored.\nEach newly substituted doubles pair shall resume play at the existing score and remain on the\ncourt until the next applicable four (4) combined-point rotation threshold is reached.\nIf the game continues beyond the initial rotation of all designated doubles pairs, the\nrotation shall return to the doubles pairs that began the game and continue in the same"},{"id":"2650bf78-47f0-4868-89b0-ece7551ff346","page":16,"score":0.5598,"content":"established order. The rotation process shall continue, as necessary, until the game is\ncompleted and a winner is determined in accordance with the applicable scoring rules.\nThe Lineups: Captains determine the lineup order, making strategy an important part of the\ngame. They must decide whether to start with their strongest team or save that team for the\nfinish. The changing matchups shift the game’s momentum, while teammates cheering adds\nenergy and excitement that can help push a team toward victory.\nWith short doubles sequences and teams ready to rotate in quickly, the Picklebreaker creates a\ntense, exciting finish to a full day of pickleball."},{"id":"09ceb070-fee5-4617-9cb9-9ed8739aef74","page":15,"score":0.5324,"content":"• Game Points: A team can win a point regardless of whether they are serving or\nreceiving except for the winning point (see next item).\n\n• Scoring Freeze and Match Point: To keep the game competitive through the final\npoint, a scoring freeze shall take eƯect whenever a team reaches one point below\nthe game-winning score. Once a team reaches the freeze point, it may score the\ngame-winning point only while serving. For example, in a game played to 25 points,\na team’s score shall initially freeze at 24. The scoring freeze applies only to the team\nthat has reached the freeze point; the opposing team may continue to score using\nnormal Rally Scoring until it also reaches the applicable freeze point. A team must\nbe serving to score the game-winning point."}],"answer":"In a Picklebreaker, every rally/serve earns a point regardless of which team served, because it always uses Rally Scoring.\n\nKey limits and mechanics:\n- The Picklebreaker is a doubles tiebreaker used when a match is tied after the day’s regular games.\n- It is played to either 15 or 25 points, depending on the league, with league-specific win-by requirements.\n- A team may score whether serving or receiving, except for the winning point.\n- When a team reaches one point below the game-winning score, its score freezes there, and that team can score the winning point only while serving. The other team may keep scoring normally until it also reaches its freeze point.\n- Doubles pairs rotate after every 4 combined points scored, using 2 pairs per team in games to 15 and 6 pairs per team in games to 25."}
